---
// The research section of shapelogic.xyz: papers and analysis from the solvers.
// The papers themselves are static pages in public/research/.
import '../../styles/landing.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Research | ShapeLogic</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Research and analysis about puzzles and shapes, and what the ShapeLogic solvers can tell us."
    />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/puzzle_icon.jpg" />
    <style>
      .banner img.logo-bar { width: auto; max-width: 100%; }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="banner">
      <a href="/"><img class="logo-bar" src="/assets/shapelogic_logo.png" alt="ShapeLogic home" width="795" height="132" /></a>
    </header>

    <main>
      <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">ShapeLogic</a> › Research</nav>
      <h1>Research</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        This section is dedicated to research and analysis about puzzles and shapes, and what the
        ShapeLogic solvers can tell us about them. Each paper starts from real solver runs and
        reports what they show.
      </p>

      <section class="blog" aria-labelledby="papers-heading">
        <h2 class="blog-heading" id="papers-heading">Papers</h2>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-golden">
          <h3 id="paper-golden">
            <a href="/research/golden-rhombus-puzzles.html">Golden Rhombus Puzzles</a>
          </h3>
          <p>
            The angles behind the rhombic triacontahedron, the 30 rhombic pyramids that assemble
            into it, and the 30-stick weave that shows the pentagons and hexagons of a truncated
            icosahedron, with the maths needed to cut every piece.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/research/golden-rhombus-puzzles.html">Read online</a>
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="post" aria-labelledby="paper-panex">
          <h3 id="paper-panex">
            <a href="/research/panex-solver-white-paper.html">Panex Solver White Paper</a>
          </h3>
          <p>
            How ShapeLogic's Panex Solver finds shortest swaps, what eight solves (levels 3 to 10)
            show about the move counts, and where the solves could be made faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/research/panex-solver-white-paper.html">Read online</a>
            ·
            <a href="/research/panex-solver-white-paper.pdf" download>Download PDF</a>
          </p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <p><a href="/">&larr; Back to ShapeLogic</a></p>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <img src="/assets/puzzle_icon.jpg" alt="" width="40" height="40" />
      <p>
        ShapeLogic is free open-source software, built and maintained by Colin Deisenroth.<br />
        Contact: <a href="mailto:shapelogic.xyz@gmail.com">shapelogic.xyz@gmail.com</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
